var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"<div id=\"landing\" class=\"landing\">\n<header class=\"landing-hero\">\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__text\">\n    <p class=\"landing-name\" style=\"--landing-name-chars: 28\">Real Cameras are Radiometric</p>\n    <h1 class=\"landing-title\">Lifting geometric camera models to radiometric ones</h1>\n    <p class=\"landing-tagline\">Ties Harder, Felix Seegräber, David Nakath, Hendrik Sauer, Kevin Köser, James Pritts\nPreprint, 2026</p>\n    <div class=\"landing-actions\">\n<a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--brand\" href=\"paper.pdf\">Paper (PDF)</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"#Paper\">arXiv (coming soon)</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://github.com/RealCamerasAreRadiometric/realcamerasareradiometric.github.io\">Code</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"docs/\">Docs</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"#Citation\">BibTeX</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__image\">\n    <div class=\"landing-hero__image-container\">\n      <img class=\"docs-light-only\" src=\"assets/landing/logo.svg\" alt=\"The radiometric camera of a fisheye lens in a cross-section. The rays from a sensor point through the aperture meet in a point on a curved focus surface and spread onto the scene.\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n      <img class=\"docs-dark-only\" src=\"assets/landing/logo_dark.svg\" alt=\"The radiometric camera of a fisheye lens in a cross-section. The rays from a sensor point through the aperture meet in a point on a curved focus surface and spread onto the scene.\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</header>\n<section class=\"landing-features\" aria-label=\"Features\">\n  <div class=\"landing-feature\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">📷</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Any central camera, lifted</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">The radiometric camera keeps the projection of a geometric model — Brown–Conrady, OpenCV, Kannala–Brandt, Double Sphere or a generic spline — and adds a finite aperture, a free-form focus surface and a generic vignetting function.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-feature\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🔁</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Calibrated by inverse rendering</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">The camera is calibrated on the RAW photosites of the same images of standard targets that a geometric calibration uses, without a feature detector, by a maximum-likelihood fit of rendered to measured radiant flux.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-feature\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🎯</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">More accurate, and physically meaningful</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">The calibrated cameras have lower reprojection errors on a held-out high-precision target, their vignetting reproduces a measured flat field, and their focus surfaces recover sharp texture from defocused images.</p>\n  </div>\n</section>\n</div>\n\n<!-- ARXIV: the arXiv URL goes in the \"arXiv\" action above and in the Paper and Citation sections below -->\n\n<!– TEASER: the paper's figures go here later (owner, 2026-11-10) –>\n\nA camera model with an aperture, a focus surface and a vignetting function around any central geometric model, calibrated from the light the sensor measures.","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Abstract","page":"Home","text":"We propose the radiometric camera, a model that lifts any central geometric camera model to a radiometric one. The lifted model keeps the projection of the geometric model and adds a finite aperture, a free-form focus surface and a generic vignetting function. We calibrate it by inverse rendering directly on the relative irradiance of the sensor, from the same images of standard calibration targets used in geometric calibration. Geometric calibration absorbs the bias of its feature detections. The proposed radiometric calibration uses no features, and our radiometric cameras have lower reprojection errors on held-out test sets. The focus surface extends defocus modeling from the thin lens to any central camera, and we use it in a first multi-view radiometric reconstruction, which recovers the in-focus reflectance of a texture from defocused images. The vignetting is estimated jointly and accurately reproduces a flat field measured through a diffuser. Because every standard camera model lifts to a radiometric one, the radiometric camera naturally and directly extends generative scene models beyond multi-view geometry to multi-view radiometric ones, where phenomena such as lighting and attenuation can be accurately estimated.","title":"Abstract"},{"category":"section","location":"#Method","page":"Home","text":"A geometric camera model maps a sensor point to one ray through its center of projection. The radiometric camera keeps that mapping and replaces the pinhole by an aperture:\n\nAperture and focus surface. The rays from a sensor point through all points of the aperture meet in one point of focus. These points form a free-form focus surface, which is learned: close to a plane for a wide-angle lens, close to a sphere for a fisheye. With a pinhole aperture, the model is the geometric model it started from.\nVignetting. A generic radial function about the principal point replaces the cos⁴ falloff of the thin lens, which real lenses do not follow.\nCalibration. The images of a calibration target are rendered and fitted to the RAW photosites under the noise model of the sensor. The formulation has no boundary terms, so every parameter of the camera, including the aperture radius and the focus surface, is differentiable, and its gauge freedoms are removed by the parameterization.","title":"Method"},{"category":"section","location":"#Results","page":"Home","text":"Geometric accuracy. On a held-out high-precision target, the radiometric refinement lowers the median reprojection error significantly for 82 % of 28 combinations of camera model, lens and calibration target, and the 95th percentile for all of them.\nVignetting. The learned vignetting follows a measured flat field for three lenses, with an RMS deviation of 0.009 to 0.042 of the value at the principal point, against 0.059 to 0.244 for the cos⁴ falloff.\nDefocus. Modeling the aperture and the focus surface lowers the median reprojection error by 25 % and 16 % against the same refinement with a pinhole aperture.\nA first multi-view radiometric reconstruction. From defocused images of a printed page, the radiometric camera recovers sharp text where a pinhole camera returns the blur of the images.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"#Paper","page":"Home","text":"<p><a href=\"paper.pdf\">The paper and its supplement (PDF)</a>, built from the\n<a href=\"https://github.com/RealCamerasAreRadiometric/realcamerasareradiometric.github.io/tree/main/paper\">manuscript\nin the repository</a>. arXiv: coming soon.</p>","title":"Paper"},{"category":"section","location":"#Code-and-documentation","page":"Home","text":"<p>The code, the experiments that reproduce the paper and the manuscript are in one repository:\n<a href=\"https://github.com/RealCamerasAreRadiometric/realcamerasareradiometric.github.io\">RealCamerasAreRadiometric/realcamerasareradiometric.github.io</a>.\nThe Julia package RadiometricCamera.jl is its subdirectory <code>RadiometricCamera/</code>;\n<a href=\"docs/\">its documentation</a> covers the installation, a tutorial, the calibration and the API.\nThe calibration is built on the <a href=\"https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit\">VisualGeometryToolkit</a>.</p>","title":"Code and documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Data","page":"Home","text":"Coming soon: the RAW images and the configurations of every experiment.","title":"Data"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-projects","page":"Home","text":"BlobBoards: robust markers for accurate pose.\nVisualGeometryToolkit: the geometry stack this work is built on.","title":"Related projects"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"@misc{realcameras2026,\n  author = {Harder, Ties and Seegr{\\\"a}ber, Felix and Nakath, David and Sauer, Hendrik and K{\\\"o}ser, Kevin and Pritts, James},\n  title  = {Real Cameras are Radiometric},\n  year   = {2026},\n}","title":"Citation"}]
}
